<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter8_creating-and-initializing-vectors">
    <title>Creating and Initializing Vectors</title>

    <p>
        A vector is a class template. The type inside the angle brackets tells
        C++ what type every element will have.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_vector_declarations"
             language="cpp" interactive="yes">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> scores;
    std::vector<std::string> names{"Amina", "Luis", "Mei"};

    scores.push_back(91);
    scores.push_back(87);

    return 0;
}
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        The first declaration creates an empty vector of integers. The second
        creates a vector of strings with three initial elements. All elements in
        one vector have the same element type.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter8_creating-and-initializing-vectors_initializers">
        <title>Initializer Lists and Size-Based Construction</title>

        <p>
            Braces can provide the initial elements directly. Parentheses can
            specify a number of elements or a repeated initial value.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_vector_initialization_forms"
                 language="cpp" interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> readings{3, 6, 9};
    std::vector<int> zeros(4);
    std::vector<int> ones(4, 1);
    std::vector<int> one_element{4};

    std::cout << readings.size() << '\n';
    std::cout << zeros.size() << '\n';
    std::cout << ones[2] << '\n';
    std::cout << one_element[0] << '\n';

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The expression <c>std::vector&lt;int&gt; zeros(4)</c> creates four
            value-initialized integers, which are zero. The expression
            <c>std::vector&lt;int&gt; ones(4, 1)</c> creates four integers, each
            initialized to 1. The expression
            <c>std::vector&lt;int&gt; one_element{4}</c> creates one element whose
            value is 4. These forms should not be confused.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter8_creating-and-initializing-vectors_copying">
        <title>Copying a Vector</title>

        <p>
            Assigning one vector to another copies its elements. After the
            assignment, the two vectors contain equal values but remain separate
            vector objects.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_copy_vector"
                 language="cpp" interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> original{10, 20, 30};
    std::vector<int> copy = original;

    copy[0] = 99;

    std::cout << original[0] << '\n';
    std::cout << copy[0] << '\n';

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The program prints 10 and then 99. Changing <c>copy</c> does not
            change <c>original</c> because the assignment created an independent
            vector with its own elements.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter8_creating-and-initializing-vectors_exercise_forms">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which declaration creates five integers, each initialized to zero?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><c>std::vector&lt;int&gt; values(5);</c></p></statement><feedback><p>Parentheses with one size argument create that many value-initialized elements.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>std::vector&lt;int&gt; values{5};</c></p></statement><feedback><p>Braces with one integer create one element whose value is 5.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>std::vector&lt;int&gt; values(0, 5);</c></p></statement><feedback><p>This requests zero elements, not five zero elements.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>std::vector&lt;int&gt; values;</c></p></statement><feedback><p>This creates an empty vector.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>
</section>
